<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erik the Red</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 950–c. 1003</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Erik Thorvaldsson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erik the Red was a Norse explorer who, outlawed from Iceland for killings, explored the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> and around 985 led settlers there, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-settlement">Eastern Settlement</a> near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaqortoq">Qaqortoq</a>. He farmed at Brattahlíð and was the leading chieftain of Norse Greenland. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leif-erikson">Leif Erikson</a> sailed on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The colonies he founded lasted almost five centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erik-the-red.jpg" alt="Erik the Red" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arngrímur Jónsson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eric_the_Red.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Norse speakers also settled in Normandy, whose name means “land of the Northmen”, in the Faroe Islands, in Iceland, and in Greenland, where <b>Erik the Red</b> founded settlements around 985; around 1000 Norse sailors reached North America, at L’Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…the North Atlantic, Norse farmers settled the Faroes, Iceland from about 870 and Greenland from about 985 under <b>Erik the Red</b>, and briefly reached Newfoundland, where the site of L’Anse aux Meadows, found in 1960 and with wood cut there in 1021, confirms the voyages to Vinland told…</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Erik the Red</b>, outlawed from Iceland, led settlers to Greenland around 985, founding the Eastern Settlement near modern Qaqortoq and a smaller Western Settlement near Nuuk.</blockquote><a href="/state/norse-greenland">Norse Greenland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaeren">Jæren</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. Died in Brattahlíð, in what is now <a href="/countries/greenland">Greenland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=58.72,5.75&amp;year=984" aria-label="Jæren on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4672 185 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Erik the Red"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5122 455h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5122 455h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=58.72,5.75&amp;year=984">What was here in 984 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am not fated to discover more countries than this in which we are now settled, and we ought not to bear one another company any longer.&#8221;</p><footer>Tale of the Greenlanders (Grænlendinga saga) &#183; tr. G. M. Gathorne-Hardy, 1921 &#183; the saga's account, written down about two centuries later; his horse had stumbled as he rode to join his son's voyage &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/76383/pg76383-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
